# Line-ending rules for Sigvault.
#
# Rationale: Rust's `cargo vendor` writes a JSON checksum for every
# vendored file. If git converts LF → CRLF on commit (Windows default
# with core.autocrlf=true), Linux CI fails with:
#   error: the listed checksum of `vendor/...` has changed
# because the actual SHA of the checked-out file no longer matches the
# `.cargo-checksum.json` entry cargo recorded.
#
# Rules:
#   1. Default — git auto-detects text vs binary, writes LF in the repo,
#      leaves the working copy alone (controlled by core.autocrlf).
#   2. Everything under vendor/ is treated as binary (-text). Git will
#      copy bytes verbatim on checkout, bypassing any autocrlf policy.
#      This is correct because vendored code is a tarball we don't edit.
#   3. Source files we DO edit get an explicit eol=lf so Windows
#      contributors don't accidentally reintroduce the same bug.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit LF for code we edit (Windows-proof).
*.rs        text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.ts        text eol=lf
*.tsx       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
LICENSE     text eol=lf

# Explicit binaries (don't let autodetection get cute).
*.wasm      binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.dll       binary
*.so        binary
*.dylib     binary

# Vendored Rust sources — treat as binary so git never rewrites bytes.
# `.cargo-checksum.json` is computed against the exact bytes cargo wrote
# when vendoring (which may be CRLF, LF, or mixed depending on upstream
# .crate tarballs). Any line-ending conversion breaks the checksum on
# any platform whose default differs from the author's.
# NOTE: this rule must come LAST so it overrides the generic `*.rs` etc.
vendor/** -text
